{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_54_41702696#10_116081575","relevance":2,"reason":"The full document is dedicated to intellectual humility and directly discusses well-being, tolerance, inquiry, and learning, but it explicitly says the evidence is unsettled and gives only cautious support rather than a firm answer to how humility leads to the good life.","learned":"Intellectual humility may contribute to the good life by improving well-being, tolerance, inquiry, and learning, but some research summaries stress that the evidence is still developing."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ humility (+ flourishing \"human flourishing\" \"good life\") (+ virtue virtues))","reason":"Search for virtue-ethics and positive-psychology sources directly connecting humility as a virtue with flourishing or the good life."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ humility (+ flourishing \"human flourishing\" \"good life\") (+ virtue virtues))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_1973574423#0_2126525093","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q3088","rank":1,"snippet":", \"segment\": \"Intellectual virtue - Wikipedia Intellectual virtue From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Jump to navigation Jump to search This article includes a list of references, related reading or external links, but its sources remain unclear because it lacks inline citations. Please help to improve this article by introducing more precise citations. ( August 2020) ( Learn how and when to remove this template message) Qualities of mind and character Intellectual virtues are qualities of mind and character that promote intellectual <cover>flourishing, critical thinking, and the pursuit of truth. They include: intellectual responsibility, perseverance, open-mindedness, empathy, integrity, intellectual courage, confidence in reason, love of truth, intellectual humility, imaginativeness, curiosity, fair-mindedness, and autonomy. So-called virtue </cover>responsibilists conceive of intellectual virtues primarily as acquired character traits, such as intellectual conscientiousness and love of knowledge. Virtue reliabilists, by contrast, think of intellectual virtues more in terms of well-functioning mental faculties such as perception, memory, and intuition. Intellectual virtues are studied extensively in both critical thinking and virtue epistemology . Contents 1 Aristotle 2 See also 3 References 4 External links Aristotle Aristotle analyzed virtues into moral and intellectual virtues. In the Posterior Analytics and Nicomachean Ethics he identified five "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"16/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_1973574423#0_2126525093","relevance":2,"reason":"The document states that intellectual virtues, including intellectual humility, promote intellectual flourishing, critical thinking, and pursuit of truth, which gives some support for humility's role in a good life. However, it is a general Wikipedia article on intellectual virtue rather than a dedicated explanation of how humility leads to the good life.","learned":"Intellectual humility is treated as an intellectual virtue that can promote intellectual flourishing, critical thinking, and pursuit of truth."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3088","reason":"The query is targeted at humility, virtue, and flourishing and may yield more dedicated sources beyond the first broad Wikipedia result."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3088"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_801831629#23_1447228390","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3088","rank":2,"snippet":"lead to greater mindfulness. That’s why the Stoics integrated daily practices of reflection – the day in review. Part 3: What Does a Stoic Look Like? The Classic Misconception – Stoics Are Unemotional The Stoics tried to use reason and training to not act out of feelings. So that they could respond with reason and virtue to whatever they felt. Domesticate your emotions, don’t pretend they don’t exist. The Stoic Love for Mankind And what if others do wrong? True Beauty Lies in Character The Ideal Stoic Personality – The Stoic Sage What Now? - The Stoicism Cheat Sheet \" , \"segment\": \"Includes good-heartedness, benevolence, public service, fair dealing. Courage or Fortitude: Includes bravery, perseverance, authenticity (honesty), confidence. Self-Discipline or Temperance: Includes orderliness, self-control, forgiveness, <cover>humility. Now, when you act according to these virtues, you progress towards the Good Life, </cover>or Eudaimonia, the ultimate goal of lif\" , \"start_char\": 9629.000000 , \"title\": \"What is Stoicism? A Definition & 10 Stoic Key Principles\" , \"url\": \"https://www.njlifehacks.com/what-is-stoicism-overview-definition-10-stoic-principles/\" }"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"17/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_801831629#23_1447228390","relevance":2,"reason":"The Stoicism article directly says humility is included under the virtue of self-discipline/temperance and that acting according to such virtues moves a person toward the Good Life or eudaimonia. However, the document is broadly about Stoicism rather than dedicated to humility's role in the good life.","learned":"In a Stoic framework, humility is part of virtuous character, and living according to virtue is what moves people toward eudaimonia, the good life."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3088","reason":"The humility-virtue-flourishing query is producing direct good-life/eudaimonia evidence, so continue to look for more dedicated sources."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3088"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_36_595481664#4_1195118864","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3088","rank":3,"snippet":"and the causes that he served became more alive and powerful after his death than during the times when he was living. Plato and Aristotle have been held in high esteem because of their intellectual achievements and the fact that their ideas have been preserved through the writings that they produced. Socrates has also been recognized as an intellectual genius, but in addition, his career in the city of Athens has come to be regarded by many persons as an outstanding example of the <cover>virtues that he advocated. His humility, intellectual honesty, devotion to the public good, and loyalty to what he believed was morally right exemplify his conception of what constitutes the good life. </cover>Because of the quality of his living, along with the abiding truth of what he taught, the story of his trial and death is something that will continue to stir the imagination of people and to win for him their admiration and respect. With reference to the trial and death of Socrates, there are four dialogs that are especially relevant. They are the Euthyphro, the Apology, the Crito, and the Phaedo. In the Euthyphro, an attempt is made to answer the question \"What is piety?\" "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"18/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_36_595481664#4_1195118864","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage directly names Socrates' humility, intellectual honesty, public devotion, and moral loyalty as virtues exemplifying his conception of the good life. However, the document appears to be about Socrates and his trial/death rather than dedicated to explaining how humility leads to the good life.","learned":"A philosophical route is that humility forms part of virtuous character, and such character can exemplify the good life through honesty, public service, and moral commitment."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ humility (+ benefits \"benefits of humility\") (+ relationships happiness wellbeing))","reason":"Search for articles explicitly organized around the benefits of humility, especially pathways through relationships, happiness, and well-being, which are likely to yield grade-3 evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ humility (+ benefits \"benefits of humility\") (+ relationships happiness wellbeing))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_43_1482386449#6_3134435952","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3089","rank":2,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_43_1482386449#6_3134435952\" , \"end_char\": 4696.000000 , \"headings\": \"Values-Based Leadership: Traits and Benefits Values-Based Leadership: Traits and Benefits What is values-based leadership? What does values-based leadership do? Setting direction Creating proactive company culture Improving individuals Expanding opportunities Key traits of a values-based leader Self-reflection Self-confidence<cover>Humility Positive influence Benefits of values-based leadership Improved communication Stronger relationships</cover>Increased productivity Helps in achieving goals Improved decision-making \" , \"segment\": \"Humility Good leaders understand who they are and where they came from. Humility for leaders is the belief that they are no different than the people who work for them. These leaders believe that every employee holds just as much value to the company as they do. Humble leaders do what it takes to lift up their teams and ensure they are well taken care of for their work. Positive influence By exemplifying the company's core values in their work, values-based leaders support their teams by serving them and ensuring they receive the best benefits ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1608274146#6_3374374297","rank":1,"relevance":2}]}}
